{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"31d2b8ebf1f69ba4ac66cb0b4080ed41f0ed5f232b522f159a0a11c2a0b67620","digest":{"component_or_area":"review-bot snapshot git fetch","key_entities":["src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py","src/rebar/_snapshot/git_fetch.py","src/rebar/review_bot/voter.py","_fetch_origin","http.lowSpeedLimit","http.lowSpeedTime","_GIT_TIMEOUT","subprocess.run","commit 206125736d","fix/12e4-fetch-abort"],"problem_keywords":["git fetch timeout","stall detection","throughput monitoring","subprocess abort","low-speed connection"],"propositions":["A stalled git fetch (accepted connection with zero bytes transferred) burns the full 300-second timeout instead of aborting in ~10 seconds due to missing http.lowSpeedLimit and http.lowSpeedTime configuration.","Git's curl handle lacks throughput-keyed abort mechanism when no low-speed options are passed, making it bounded only by wall-clock timeout rather than data transfer rate.","A legitimately slow but progressing fetch (e.g., 2000 B/s on an 80 MB repo over 90 seconds) must not be aborted by the fix, requiring throughput-based detection rather than wall-time-based detection.","Transient low-speed aborts should be retried in-process a bounded number of times to self-heal within a single review, while non-low-speed failures (auth, bad ref) must not be retried."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787320289078832001,"uuid":"a87a6b45-40c2-45fe-9a8d-4bf8bbf95497"}